answer:
If the Commission concludes that the arguments submitted by the provider are not sufficiently substantiated and the provider fails to demonstrate that the model does not present systemic risks due to its specific characteristics, the Commission "shall reject those arguments, and the general-purpose AI model shall be considered to be a general-purpose AI model with systemic risk" [Art. 52(3)].

Consequently, the provider becomes subject to the obligations for general-purpose AI models with systemic risk, which include [Art. 55(1)]:
* Performing model evaluations under standardised protocols reflecting the state of the art, including conducting and documenting adversarial testing to identify and mitigate systemic risks [Art. 55(1)(a)];
* Assessing and mitigating possible systemic risks at Union level stemming from development, market placement, or use [Art. 55(1)(b)];
* Tracking, documenting, and reporting without undue delay to the AI Office (and national competent authorities, as appropriate) relevant information on serious incidents and corrective measures [Art. 55(1)(c)];
* Ensuring an adequate level of cybersecurity protection for the model and its physical infrastructure [Art. 55(1)(d)];
* Complying with these requirements in addition to the obligations listed in Articles 53 and 54 [Art. 55(1)].

Under Article 92(4) of the EU Artificial Intelligence Act, a request for access must include the following details [Art. 92(4)]:

* The legal basis [Art. 92(4)];
* The purpose and reasons of the request [Art. 92(4)];
* The period within which the access is to be provided [Art. 92(4)];
* The fines provided for in Article 101 for failure to provide access [Art. 92(4)].

Yes. High-risk AI systems must be designed and developed—including with appropriate human-machine interface tools—in such a way that they can be effectively overseen by natural persons during the period in which they are in use [Art. 14(1)]. 

Key details and conditions regarding this supervision include:

* **Purpose:** Human oversight aims to "prevent or minimise the risks to health, safety or fundamental rights" when the system is used as intended or under reasonably foreseeable misuse [Art. 14(2)].
* **Implementation of measures:** Oversight measures must be commensurate with the system's risks, autonomy, and context of use, and must be built into the system by the provider and/or identified by the provider for implementation by the deployer [Art. 14(3)]. Deployers must assign this oversight to natural persons with the "necessary competence, training and authority, as well as the necessary support" [Art. 26(2)].
* **Capabilities of overseers:** Natural persons assigned to oversight must be enabled to:
  * Understand the system’s capacities and limitations and monitor its operation to detect anomalies, dysfunctions, and unexpected performance [Art. 14(4)(a)];
  * Remain aware of automation bias (the tendency to automatically rely or over-rely on outputs) [Art. 14(4)(b)];
  * Correctly interpret outputs [Art. 14(4)(c)];
  * Decide to not use the system, or to disregard, override, or reverse its output [Art. 14(4)(d)];
  * Intervene or interrupt operation via a "stop" button or similar procedure to halt the system safely [Art. 14(4)(e)].
* **Specific verification requirement:** For high-risk AI systems referred to in point 1(a) of Annex III, deployers must ensure that no action or decision is taken based on the system's identification unless it has been separately verified and confirmed by at least two competent, trained natural persons [Art. 14(5)]. This two-person rule does not apply to law enforcement, migration, border control, or asylum uses if Union or national law considers it disproportionate [Art. 14(5)].

Yes. Providers of high-risk AI systems are expressly required to keep the documentation referred to in Article 18 [Art. 16(d)]. 

Specifically, documentation record-keeping obligations include:

* **General documentation-keeping obligation and retention period:** The provider must keep certain documentation at the disposal of national competent authorities for a period ending 10 years after the high-risk AI system has been placed on the market or put into service [Art. 18(1)]. This includes:
  * The technical documentation referred to in Article 11 [Art. 18(1)(a)], which must be drawn up before placing the system on the market or putting it into service and kept up to date [Art. 11(1)];
  * Documentation concerning the quality management system referred to in Article 17 [Art. 18(1)(b)];
  * Documentation concerning changes approved by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(c)];
  * Decisions and other documents issued by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(d)];
  * The EU declaration of conformity referred to in Article 47 [Art. 18(1)(e)], which must also be kept at the disposal of national competent authorities for 10 years after placing on the market or putting into service [Art. 47(1)].

* **Quality management system requirement:** Providers must include "systems and procedures for record-keeping of all relevant documentation and information" within their quality management system [Art. 17(1)(k)].

* **Special rule for financial institutions:** Providers that are financial institutions subject to internal governance requirements under Union financial services law must maintain the technical documentation as part of the documentation kept under that relevant law [Art. 18(3)].

Where a market surveillance authority takes a decision regarding real-world testing (such as suspending, terminating, or requiring modifications to the testing) or issues an objection to it, the decision or objection must indicate [Art. 76(3)], [Art. 76(4)]:

* The grounds for the decision or objection [Art. 76(4)];
* How the provider or prospective provider can challenge the decision or objection [Art. 76(4)].

The European Data Protection Supervisor may impose administrative fines on Union institutions, bodies, offices, and agencies falling within the scope of the Regulation [Art. 100(1)].

When deciding whether to impose an administrative fine and when deciding on its amount in each individual case, "all relevant circumstances of the specific situation shall be taken into account" and due regard must be given to the following factors:

* **Nature and impact:** The nature, gravity, and duration of the infringement and its consequences, taking into account the purpose of the AI system concerned, as well as, where appropriate, the number of affected persons and the level of damage suffered by them [Art. 100(1)(a)];
* **Responsibility:** The degree of responsibility of the Union institution, body, office, or agency, taking into account technical and organisational measures implemented by them [Art. 100(1)(b)];
* **Mitigation:** Any action taken by the Union institution, body, office, or agency to mitigate the damage suffered by affected persons [Art. 100(1)(c)];
* **Cooperation:** The degree of cooperation with the European Data Protection Supervisor to remedy the infringement and mitigate its possible adverse effects, including compliance with any measures previously ordered against them regarding the same subject matter [Art. 100(1)(d)];
* **Prior conduct:** Any similar previous infringements by the Union institution, body, office, or agency [Art. 100(1)(e)];
* **Notification:** The manner in which the infringement became known to the European Data Protection Supervisor, particularly whether, and to what extent, the institution, body, office, or agency notified the infringement [Art. 100(1)(f)];
* **Budget:** The annual budget of the Union institution, body, office, or agency [Art. 100(1)(g)].

Yes. If you are a provider of a high-risk AI system, you are required to establish, implement, document, and maintain a risk management system [Art. 9(1)], [Art. 16(a)], [Art. 16(c)]. 

Under the Act, the criteria and requirements for this process are:
* It must be a "continuous iterative process planned and run throughout the entire lifecycle of a high-risk AI system, requiring regular systematic review and updating" [Art. 9(2)].
* It must comprise the following steps:
  * Identifying and analysing known and reasonably foreseeable risks to health, safety, or fundamental rights when the system is used as intended [Art. 9(2)(a)];
  * Estimating and evaluating risks emerging from intended use and conditions of "reasonably foreseeable misuse" [Art. 9(2)(b)];
  * Evaluating other risks arising from post-market monitoring data gathered under Article 72 [Art. 9(2)(c)];
  * Adopting "appropriate and targeted risk management measures" to address identified risks [Art. 9(2)(d)].
* This risk management system must also be included as an aspect of the provider's quality management system [Art. 16(c)], [Art. 17(1)(g)].

This explanation does not constitute legal advice.

Under the EU AI Act, providers of general-purpose AI models must draw up and keep up to date technical documentation containing, at a minimum, the information set out in Annex XI to be provided upon request to the AI Office and national competent authorities [Art. 53(1)(a)]. 

This documentation must contain, as appropriate to the size and risk profile of the model, the following basic details [Annex XI, Section 1]:

* **A general description of the model**, including [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1]:
  * The tasks the model is intended to perform and the type and nature of AI systems in which it can be integrated [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1(a)];
  * Applicable acceptable use policies [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1(b)];
  * Date of release and methods of distribution [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1(c)];
  * Architecture and number of parameters [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1(d)];
  * Modality (e.g. text, image) and format of inputs and outputs [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1(e)];
  * The licence [Annex XI, Section 1, point 1(f)].

* **Development and model elements**, including [Annex XI, Section 1, point 2]:
  * Technical means required for integration into AI systems (e.g. instructions of use, infrastructure, tools) [Annex XI, Section 1, point 2(a)];
  * Design specifications of the model and training process (training methodologies, key design choices, rationale, assumptions, what the model optimises for, and parameter relevance) [Annex XI, Section 1, point 2(b)];
  * Information on data used for training, testing, and validation (type, provenance, curation methodologies, number of data points, scope, characteristics, how data was obtained/selected, and measures to detect unsuitable data sources and identifiable biases) [Annex XI, Section 1, point 2(c)];
  * Computational resources used (e.g. number of floating point operations), training time, and training details [Annex XI, Section 1, point 2(d)];
  * Known or estimated energy consumption (which may be based on computational resources if unknown) [Annex XI, Section 1, point 2(e)].

If the general-purpose AI model carries **systemic risk**, providers must additionally include [Annex XI, Section 2, points 1–3]:
* A detailed description of evaluation strategies, metrics, criteria, limitations identification, and results [Annex XI, Section 2, point 1];
* Adversarial testing measures (e.g. red teaming) and model adaptations (such as alignment and fine-tuning), where applicable [Annex XI, Section 2, point 2];
* System architecture details explaining how software components integrate and feed into each other, where applicable [Annex XI, Section 2, point 3].

*(Note: Providers must also prepare downstream transparency documentation containing elements set out in Annex XII [Art. 53(1)(b), Annex XII].)*

The provided provisions do not address whether testing bodies based outside the EU can be approved to check AI systems. 

The provided excerpts exclusively cover the rules for providers or prospective providers conducting the testing of high-risk AI systems in real-world conditions outside AI regulatory sandboxes [Art. 60(1)], including:
* Establishment requirements, specifying that a provider or prospective provider conducting such testing must be "established in the Union or has appointed a legal representative who is established in the Union" [Art. 60(4)(d)];
* The roles, powers, and approval procedures of market surveillance authorities in Member States [Art. 60(4)(a)], [Art. 60(4)(b)], [Art. 60(6)];
* Internal oversight by providers and deployers [Art. 60(4)(j)]; and
* Rules regarding data transfers to third countries [Art. 60(4)(e)].

Based on the provided excerpts, the provider must ensure logging capabilities and documentation tracking after placing a high-risk AI system on the market or putting it into service:

* **Logging and Traceability:** Logging capabilities must enable the recording of events relevant for:
  * identifying situations that may result in the system presenting a risk (within the meaning of Article 79(1)) or in a substantial modification [Art. 12(2)(a)];
  * facilitating post-market monitoring referred to in Article 72 [Art. 12(2)(b)]; and
  * monitoring the operation of high-risk AI systems referred to in Article 26(5) [Art. 12(2)(c)].
* **Testing and Quality Management:** The quality management system must include examination, test, and validation procedures to be carried out after the development of the system and specify their frequency [Art. 17(1)(d)], as well as systems and procedures for record-keeping of all relevant documentation and information [Art. 17(1)(k)].
* **Documentation Retention (10 years):** For a period ending 10 years after the system has been placed on the market or put into service, the provider must keep at the disposal of national competent authorities:
  * the technical documentation referred to in Article 11 [Art. 18(1)(a)];
  * quality management system documentation referred to in Article 17 [Art. 18(1)(b)];
  * documentation regarding changes approved by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(c)];
  * decisions and other documents issued by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(d)];
  * the EU declaration of conformity [Art. 18(1)(e)], which must be drawn up in written machine-readable, physical, or electronically signed format and submitted to authorities upon request [Art. 47(1)].

**Exceptions:**
* The provided excerpts do not specify any exceptions to the post-release event logging or 10-year documentation retention rules. 
* Regarding pre-market registration in the EU database, an exception exists for "high-risk AI systems referred to in point 2 of Annex III" [Art. 49(1)], and a provider who assesses that an Annex III system is not high-risk is not subject to high-risk classification but must document its assessment and register under Article 49(2) [Art. 6(4)]. The excerpts do not provide other exceptions.

If you are a deployer required to register the use of a high-risk AI system under Article 49(3) (applicable to public authorities, Union institutions, bodies, offices, or agencies, or persons acting on their behalf, for systems listed in Annex III other than point 2) [Art. 49(3)], you must provide and keep up to date the following information in the EU database [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5]:

* The name, address, and contact details of the deployer [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5];
* The name, address, and contact details of the person submitting information on behalf of the deployer [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5];
* The URL of the entry of the AI system in the EU database by its provider [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5];
* A summary of the findings of the fundamental rights impact assessment conducted in accordance with Article 27 [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5];
* Where applicable, a summary of the data protection impact assessment carried out in accordance with Article 35 of Regulation (EU) 2016/679 or Article 27 of Directive (EU) 2016/680 as specified in Article 26(8) [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5].

Additionally, the personal data contained in the database includes the names and contact details of natural persons who are responsible for registering the system and have the legal authority to represent the deployer [Art. 71(5)]. Note that if you find the system has not been registered in the database by its provider, you must not use it and must inform the provider or distributor [Art. 26(8)].

This is not legal advice.

Yes. National public authorities or bodies that protect fundamental rights are required to treat any information or documentation they obtain pursuant to Article 77 in accordance with the confidentiality obligations set out in Article 78 [Art. 77(4)].

Under these obligations, they must, in accordance with Union or national law, respect the confidentiality of information and data obtained in carrying out their tasks and activities to protect, in particular [Art. 78(1)]:
* intellectual property rights and confidential business information or trade secrets, including source code (except as referred to in Article 5 of Directive (EU) 2016/943) [Art. 78(1)(a)];
* the effective implementation of the Regulation, specifically for inspections, investigations, or audits [Art. 78(1)(b)];
* public and national security interests [Art. 78(1)(c)];
* the conduct of criminal or administrative proceedings [Art. 78(1)(d)];
* information classified pursuant to Union or national law [Art. 78(1)(e)].

A general-purpose AI model is classified as presenting a systemic risk if it meets either of the following conditions:

* **High impact capabilities:** Evaluated on the basis of appropriate technical tools and methodologies, including indicators and benchmarks [Art. 51(1)(a)]. A model is **presumed** to have high impact capabilities when the cumulative amount of computation used for its training measured in floating point operations is greater than $10^{25}$ [Art. 51(2)].
* **Commission decision (equivalent capabilities or impact):** Based on a decision of the Commission (ex officio or following a qualified alert from the scientific panel), when it has capabilities or an impact equivalent to high impact capabilities, having regard to specific criteria [Art. 51(1)(b)], [Art. 52(4)].

For determining equivalent capabilities or impact, the Commission takes into account the following criteria set out in Annex XIII [Art. 51(1)(b)], [Annex XIII]:
* The number of parameters of the model [Annex XIII, point (a)];
* The quality or size of the data set (for example, measured through tokens) [Annex XIII, point (b)];
* The amount of computation used for training the model, measured in floating point operations or indicated by a combination of variables such as estimated cost, estimated time, or estimated energy consumption for training [Annex XIII, point (c)];
* The input and output modalities (such as text to text, text to image, multi-modality), state-of-the-art thresholds for determining high-impact capabilities for each modality, and specific input/output types (e.g., biological sequences) [Annex XIII, point (d)];
* Benchmarks and capability evaluations, including the number of tasks performed without additional training, adaptability to learn new and distinct tasks, level of autonomy, scalability, and access to tools [Annex XIII, point (e)];
* Whether it has a high impact on the internal market due to reach, which is presumed when made available to at least 10,000 registered business users established in the Union [Annex XIII, point (f)];
* The number of registered end-users [Annex XIII, point (g)].

In general, systemic risk is defined by whether the model's high-impact capabilities have a significant impact on the Union market due to reach, or actual or reasonably foreseeable negative effects on public health, safety, public security, fundamental rights, or society as a whole, propagating at scale across the value chain [Art. 3(65)].

Under the EU AI Act, importers must indicate the following information on the high-risk AI system and on its packaging or its accompanying documentation, where applicable:

* Their name, registered trade name, or registered trade mark [Art. 23(3)];
* The address at which they can be contacted [Art. 23(3)].

Based on the provided excerpts, a provider has the following obligations regarding its authorised representative:

* **Enabling the representative:** For high-risk AI systems, the provider must "enable its authorised representative to perform the tasks specified in the mandate received from the provider" [Art. 22(2)].
* **Mandating required tasks for high-risk AI systems:** The provider must give a mandate that empowers the authorised representative to:
  * Be addressed by competent authorities, in addition to or instead of the provider, on all compliance issues [Art. 22(3)];
  * Verify that the EU declaration of conformity and technical documentation are drawn up and an appropriate conformity assessment procedure was carried out [Art. 22(3)(a)];
  * Keep available for competent and national authorities (for 10 years after placing on the market or putting into service) the provider's contact details, the EU declaration of conformity, technical documentation, and any notified body certificates [Art. 22(3)(b)];
  * Provide competent authorities, upon reasoned request, with necessary conformity information and documentation, including access to automatically generated logs to the extent they are under the provider's control [Art. 22(3)(c)];
  * Cooperate with competent authorities to reduce and mitigate risks posed by the system [Art. 22(3)(d)];
  * Comply with registration obligations under Article 49(1), or, if the provider registers itself, ensure that the information referred to in point 3 of Section A of Annex VIII is correct [Art. 22(3)(e)].
* **Mandating required tasks for general-purpose AI models:** The provider must establish a mandate empowering the authorised representative to:
  * Verify that Annex XI technical documentation is drawn up and obligations under Article 53 (and Article 55, where applicable) are fulfilled [Art. 54(3)(a)];
  * Keep a copy of the Annex XI technical documentation and the provider's contact details at the disposal of the AI Office and national competent authorities for 10 years after the model has been placed on the market [Art. 54(3)(b)];
  * Provide the AI Office, upon reasoned request, with all documentation and information needed to demonstrate compliance [Art. 54(3)(c)];
  * Cooperate with the AI Office and competent authorities in any actions taken regarding the model [Art. 54(3)(d)].

Upon a request by national competent authorities, providers of high-risk AI systems must provide or make accessible the following documentation and information:

* **Conformity information and documentation:** All the information and documentation necessary to demonstrate the conformity of the high-risk AI system with the requirements set out in Section 2, provided upon a reasoned request in a language easily understood by the authority in one of the official languages of the Union institutions indicated by the Member State concerned [Art. 21(1)], [Art. 16(k)].
* **EU declaration of conformity:** A copy of the written EU declaration of conformity must be submitted upon request [Art. 47(1)]. Providers must also keep this declaration at the disposal of national competent authorities for 10 years after the system has been placed on the market or put into service [Art. 47(1)], [Art. 18(1)(e)].
* **Automatically generated logs:** Access to the automatically generated logs referred to in Article 12(1), upon a reasoned request, to the extent such logs are under their control [Art. 21(2)].
* **Documentation kept at disposal:** For a period ending 10 years after the system has been placed on the market or put into service, providers must keep at the disposal of national competent authorities [Art. 18(1)]:
  * The technical documentation referred to in Article 11 [Art. 18(1)(a)];
  * Documentation concerning the quality management system referred to in Article 17 [Art. 18(1)(b)];
  * Documentation concerning changes approved by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(c)];
  * Decisions and other documents issued by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(d)];
  * The EU declaration of conformity referred to in Article 47 [Art. 18(1)(e)].

Under Article 66(e) of the AI Act, at the request of the Commission or on its own initiative, the Board may issue recommendations and written opinions on "any relevant matters related to the implementation of this Regulation and to its consistent and effective application" [Art. 66(e)]. 

This specifically includes:
* The development and application of codes of conduct and codes of practice under the Regulation, as well as the Commission’s guidelines [Art. 66(e)(i)];
* The evaluation and review of the Regulation under Article 112, including regarding:
  * serious incident reports referred to in Article 73,
  * the functioning of the EU database referred to in Article 71,
  * the preparation of delegated or implementing acts, and
  * possible alignments of the Regulation with the Union harmonisation legislation listed in Annex I [Art. 66(e)(ii)];
* Technical specifications or existing standards concerning the requirements set out in Chapter III, Section 2 [Art. 66(e)(iii)];
* The use of harmonised standards or common specifications referred to in Articles 40 and 41 [Art. 66(e)(iv)];
* Trends such as European global competitiveness in AI, the uptake of AI in the Union, and digital skills development [Art. 66(e)(v)];
* Trends on the evolving typology of AI value chains, particularly regarding resulting implications for accountability [Art. 66(e)(vi)];
* The potential need for amendments to Annex III under Article 7, and the potential need for possible revision of Article 5 under Article 112, taking into account available evidence and the latest technological developments [Art. 66(e)(vii)].

Additionally, the Board may provide opinions to the Commission on qualified alerts regarding general-purpose AI models [Art. 66(n)].

Under Article 10(5) of the EU Artificial Intelligence Act, providers of high-risk AI systems may process special categories of personal data exceptionally and only "to the extent that it is strictly necessary for the purpose of ensuring bias detection and correction in relation to the high-risk AI systems in accordance with paragraph (2), points (f) and (g)" [Art. 10(5)].

Such processing is subject to appropriate safeguards for fundamental rights and freedoms, and must comply with Regulations (EU) 2016/679 and (EU) 2018/1725 and Directive (EU) 2016/680 [Art. 10(5)]. In addition, **all** of the following conditions must be met [Art. 10(5)]:

* **Non-substitutability:** the bias detection and correction cannot be effectively fulfilled by processing other data, including synthetic or anonymised data [Art. 10(5)(a)];
* **Security and privacy measures:** the data are subject to technical limitations on re-use, as well as state-of-the-art security and privacy-preserving measures, including pseudonymisation [Art. 10(5)(b)];
* **Strict access controls:** the data are secured, protected, and subject to suitable safeguards, including strict controls and documentation of access, to prevent misuse and ensure that only authorised persons subject to confidentiality obligations have access [Art. 10(5)(c)];
* **No third-party transfer:** the data are not to be transmitted, transferred, or otherwise accessed by other parties [Art. 10(5)(d)];
* **Data deletion:** the data are deleted once the bias has been corrected or the data reaches the end of its retention period, whichever comes first [Art. 10(5)(e)];
* **Documentation:** records of processing activities include the specific reasons why processing this data was strictly necessary to detect and correct biases and why that objective could not be achieved using other data [Art. 10(5)(f)].

Where a high-risk AI system does not use techniques involving the training of AI models, these provisions apply only to the testing data sets [Art. 10(6)].

Under the EU Artificial Intelligence Act, high-risk AI systems must technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the system's lifetime [Art. 12(1)]. 

To ensure traceability appropriate to the system's intended purpose, logging capabilities must enable the recording of events relevant for:
* "identifying situations that may result in the high-risk AI system presenting a risk within the meaning of Article 79(1) or in a substantial modification" [Art. 12(2)(a)];
* "facilitating the post-market monitoring referred to in Article 72" [Art. 12(2)(b)]; and
* "monitoring the operation of high-risk AI systems referred to in Article 26(5)" [Art. 12(2)(c)].

Additionally, for high-risk AI systems referred to in point 1(a) of Annex III, logging capabilities must provide, at a minimum, the recording of:
* the period of each use (start date and time, and end date and time) [Art. 12(3)(a)];
* the reference database against which input data has been checked [Art. 12(3)(b)];
* the input data for which the search led to a match [Art. 12(3)(c)]; and
* the identification of the natural persons involved in verifying the results under Article 14(5) [Art. 12(3)(d)].

The details and documents required for registering a high-risk AI system depend on your role (provider or deployer) and the applicable provision of Article 49:

### 1. Registration by providers under Article 49(1)
Providers of high-risk AI systems must provide and keep up to date the following details and documents [Annex VIII, Section A, points 1–5]:
* **Contact and representative details:** The name, address, and contact details of the provider [Annex VIII, Section A, points 1–5]; where submitted by another person on behalf of the provider, that person's name, address, and contact details [Annex VIII, Section A, points 1–5]; and the name, address, and contact details of the authorised representative, where applicable [Annex VIII, Section A, points 1–5].
* **System identification and purpose:** The AI system trade name and any additional unambiguous reference allowing identification and traceability [Annex VIII, Section A, points 1–5], along with a description of the intended purpose and of the components and functions supported through the AI system [Annex VIII, Section A, points 1–5].
* **Operational information and status:** A basic and concise description of the information used by the system (data, inputs) and its operating logic [Annex VIII, Section A, points 6–11], and the status of the AI system (on the market, or in service; no longer placed on the market/in service, recalled) [Annex VIII, Section A, points 6–11].
* **Market information:** Any Member States in which the system has been placed on the market, put into service, or made available in the Union [Annex VIII, Section A, points 6–11].
* **Required documents and certificates:** 
  * A copy of the EU declaration of conformity referred to in Article 47 [Annex VIII, Section A, points 6–11].
  * Where applicable, the type, number, and expiry date of the certificate issued by the notified body, the name or identification number of that notified body, and a scanned copy of that certificate [Annex VIII, Section A, points 6–11].

*(Note: Personal data in the EU database includes the names and contact details of natural persons who are responsible for registering the system and legally authorized to represent the provider or deployer [Art. 71(5)].)*

---

### 2. Registration by providers under Article 49(2) (Annex III systems considered not high-risk)
If a provider considers an AI system referred to in Annex III to be not high-risk under Article 6(3), it must document its assessment before placing it on the market or putting it into service, and submit the following under Article 49(2) [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6], [Art. 6(4)]:
* Name, address, and contact details of the provider [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6];
* Name, address, and contact details of the person submitting information on the provider’s behalf, if applicable [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6];
* Name, address, and contact details of the authorised representative, where applicable [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6];
* The AI system trade name and any additional unambiguous reference allowing identification and traceability [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6];
* A description of the intended purpose of the AI system [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6];
* The condition or conditions under Article 6(3) based on which the system is considered not high-risk [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6].

---

### 3. Registration by deployers under Article 49(3)
Deployers registering high-risk AI systems must submit and keep up to date [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5]:
* The name, address, and contact details of the deployer [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5];
* The name, address, and contact details of the person submitting information on behalf of the deployer [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5];
* The URL of the entry of the AI system in the EU database by its provider [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5];
* A summary of the findings of the fundamental rights impact assessment conducted in accordance with Article 27 [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5];
* Where applicable, a summary of the data protection impact assessment carried out in accordance with Article 35 of Regulation (EU) 2016/679 or Article 27 of Directive (EU) 2016/680 as specified in Article 26(8) [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5].

---

### 4. Registration for testing in real world conditions (Article 60)
For high-risk AI systems listed in Annex III undergoing testing in real world conditions, the registration details comprise [Annex IX]:
* A Union-wide unique single identification number of the testing [Annex IX];
* Name and contact details of the provider/prospective provider and deployers involved [Annex IX];
* A brief description of the system, its intended purpose, and other necessary identification information [Annex IX];
* A summary of the main characteristics of the testing plan [Annex IX];
* Information on the suspension or termination of the testing [Annex IX].

This explanation reflects the Act's statutory criteria for your situation; this is not legal advice.

Yes. The Commission is required to ensure that all sensitive information obtained in the course of its investigations into the competence of a notified body is treated confidentially in accordance with Article 78 [Art. 37(3)].

Under these rules, the Commission must, in accordance with Union or national law, respect the confidentiality of information and data obtained in carrying out its tasks and activities so as to protect, in particular:
* intellectual property rights, confidential business information, or trade secrets (including source code), subject to specific exceptions under Directive (EU) 2016/943 [Art. 78(1)(a)];
* the effective implementation of the Regulation, particularly for inspections, investigations, or audits [Art. 78(1)(b)];
* public and national security interests [Art. 78(1)(c)];
* the conduct of criminal or administrative proceedings [Art. 78(1)(d)];
* information classified pursuant to Union or national law [Art. 78(1)(e)].

Yes. Notifying authorities are required to safeguard the confidentiality of the information that they obtain, in accordance with Article 78 [Art. 28(6)]. 

Under these rules, entities involved in the application of the Regulation must respect the confidentiality of information and data in accordance with Union or national law, specifically to protect:
* intellectual property rights and confidential business information or trade secrets (including source code), subject to specific exceptions under Directive (EU) 2016/943 [Art. 78(1)(a)];
* the effective implementation of the Regulation, particularly for inspections, investigations, or audits [Art. 78(1)(b)];
* public and national security interests [Art. 78(1)(c)];
* the conduct of criminal or administrative proceedings [Art. 78(1)(d)];
* information classified under Union or national law [Art. 78(1)(e)].

However, when requested, the notifying authority must provide the Commission with all relevant information relating to the notification or the maintenance of the competence of the notified body concerned [Art. 37(2)].

Under the EU Artificial Intelligence Act, high-risk AI systems must be resilient against attempts by unauthorised third parties to alter their use, outputs, or performance by exploiting system vulnerabilities [Art. 15(5)]. 

Technical solutions to address AI-specific vulnerabilities must include, where appropriate, measures to prevent, detect, respond to, resolve, and control for:
* Attacks trying to manipulate the training data set ("data poisoning") [Art. 15(5)], [Recital 76];
* Attacks trying to manipulate pre-trained components used in training ("model poisoning") [Art. 15(5)];
* Inputs designed to cause the AI model to make a mistake ("adversarial examples or model evasion") [Art. 15(5)], or adversarial attacks generally [Recital 76];
* Confidentiality attacks [Art. 15(5)] (such as membership inference) [Recital 76];
* Model flaws [Art. 15(5)].

In addition, systems must be protected against attacks that exploit vulnerabilities in the AI system’s digital assets or its underlying ICT infrastructure [Recital 76].

Where a market surveillance authority has been informed by the prospective provider, the provider, or any third party of a serious incident (or has other grounds for considering that the conditions set out in Articles 60 and 61 are not met), it may take either of the following decisions on its territory, as appropriate [Art. 76(3)]:

* To suspend or terminate the testing in real world conditions [Art. 76(3)(a)];
* To require the provider or prospective provider and the deployer or prospective deployer to modify any aspect of the testing in real world conditions [Art. 76(3)(b)].

Additionally, to ensure the safe development of testing in real world conditions, market surveillance authorities have the powers to [Art. 60(6)]:
* Require providers and prospective providers to provide information [Art. 60(6)];
* Carry out unannounced remote or on-site inspections [Art. 60(6)];
* Perform checks on the conduct of the testing in real world conditions and the related high-risk AI systems [Art. 60(6)].

Under the EU AI Act, if a provider of a high-risk AI system or its authorised representative established on a Member State's territory goes bankrupt or ceases its activity before the mandatory 10-year storage period ends:

* **Member State rules apply:** "Each Member State shall determine conditions under which the documentation referred to in paragraph 1 remains at the disposal of the national competent authorities for the period indicated in that paragraph" [Art. 18(2)].
* **Documentation covered:** This refers to the documentation kept for 10 years after the system is placed on the market or put into service [Art. 18(1)], specifically:
  * Technical documentation [Art. 18(1)(a)];
  * Quality management system documentation [Art. 18(1)(b)];
  * Documentation concerning changes approved by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(c)];
  * Decisions and other documents issued by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(d)];
  * The EU declaration of conformity [Art. 18(1)(e)].

When the Commission requests information from a general-purpose AI model provider under Article 91, the request must state and include the following details [Art. 91(4)]:

* The legal basis of the request [Art. 91(4)];
* The purpose of the request [Art. 91(4)];
* A specification of what information is required [Art. 91(4)];
* A set period within which the information is to be provided [Art. 91(4)]; and
* An indication of the fines provided for in Article 101 for supplying incorrect, incomplete, or misleading information [Art. 91(4)].

Yes. When performing their tasks, national competent authorities, market surveillance authorities, and national public authorities or bodies protecting fundamental rights must act in accordance with the confidentiality obligations set out in Article 78 [Art. 70(5)], [Art. 77(4)], [Art. 78(1)].

Under these obligations:
* Authorities must, in accordance with Union or national law, respect the confidentiality of information and data obtained in carrying out their tasks and activities to protect, in particular:
  * Intellectual property rights and confidential business information or trade secrets, including source code (except in cases under Article 5 of Directive (EU) 2016/943) [Art. 78(1)(a)];
  * The effective implementation of the Regulation (especially for inspections, investigations, or audits) [Art. 78(1)(b)];
  * Public and national security interests [Art. 78(1)(c)];
  * The conduct of criminal or administrative proceedings [Art. 78(1)(d)];
  * Information classified pursuant to Union or national law [Art. 78(1)(e)].
* Information exchanged on a confidential basis between national competent authorities, or between them and the Commission, must not be disclosed without prior consultation of the originating authority and the deployer when high-risk AI systems referred to in point 1, 6, or 7 of Annex III are used by law enforcement, border control, immigration, or asylum authorities and disclosure would jeopardise public and national security interests [Art. 78(3)].
* Such exchanges of information must not cover sensitive operational data related to the activities of law enforcement, border control, immigration, or asylum authorities [Art. 78(3)]. 
* Where law enforcement, immigration, or asylum authorities provide such systems, technical documentation must remain on their premises, and only market surveillance authority staff holding the appropriate level of security clearance are permitted access to it or its copies [Art. 78(3)].

Prior to participating in testing in real world conditions, subjects must be duly provided with "concise, clear, relevant, and understandable information" regarding [Art. 61(1)]:

* **Nature, objectives, and inconvenience:** The nature and objectives of the testing and the possible inconvenience that may be linked to their participation [Art. 61(1)(a)];
* **Conditions and duration:** The conditions under which the testing is conducted, including the expected duration of their participation [Art. 61(1)(b)];
* **Rights and guarantees:** Their rights and guarantees, specifically their right to refuse to participate and to withdraw at any time "without any resulting detriment and without having to provide any justification" [Art. 61(1)(c)];
* **Reversal arrangements:** The arrangements for requesting the reversal or disregarding of the AI system's predictions, recommendations, or decisions [Art. 61(1)(d)];
* **Identification and contact details:** The Union-wide unique single identification number of the testing and the contact details of the provider or its legal representative to obtain further information [Art. 61(1)(e)].

More broadly, informed consent requires that the subject be informed of "all aspects of the testing that are relevant to the subject’s decision to participate" [Art. 3(59)]. In addition, the informed consent must be dated and documented, and a copy must be provided to the subjects or their legal representative [Art. 61(2)].

Under Annex XII, providers of general-purpose AI models must share technical documentation containing at least the following details with downstream providers who integrate the model into their AI systems [Annex XII]:

* **A general description of the model**, including:
  * The tasks the model is intended to perform and the type and nature of AI systems into which it can be integrated [Annex XII, point 1(a)];
  * Applicable acceptable use policies [Annex XII, point 1(b)];
  * Release date and distribution methods [Annex XII, point 1(c)];
  * How the model interacts, or can interact, with hardware or software that is not part of the model, where applicable [Annex XII, point 1(d)];
  * Versions of relevant software related to its use, where applicable [Annex XII, point 1(e)];
  * Architecture and number of parameters [Annex XII, point 1(f)];
  * Modality (e.g., text, image) and format of inputs and outputs [Annex XII, point 1(g)];
  * The licence for the model [Annex XII, point 1(h)].

* **A description of the elements of the model and its development process**, including:
  * Technical means required to integrate the model into AI systems (e.g., instructions for use, infrastructure, tools) [Annex XII, point 2(a)];
  * Modality and format of inputs and outputs, as well as their maximum size (e.g., context window length) [Annex XII, point 2(b)];
  * Information on data used for training, testing, and validation, where applicable, including data type, provenance, and curation methodologies [Annex XII, point 2(c)].

This information must enable downstream providers to understand the model's capabilities and limitations and comply with their obligations, provided without prejudice to the need to protect intellectual property rights, confidential business information, or trade secrets [Art. 53(1)(b)].

The information an EU Member State must share when dealing with an AI system that presents a risk depends on the specific situation:

* **When a high-risk AI system complies with the Regulation but still presents a risk:** The Member State must immediately inform the Commission and other Member States of its finding [Art. 82(3)]. This information must include all available details, specifically:
  * The data necessary to identify the AI system concerned [Art. 82(3)];
  * The origin and supply chain of the AI system [Art. 82(3)];
  * The nature of the risk involved [Art. 82(3)];
  * The nature and duration of the national measures taken [Art. 82(3)].

* **When a non-compliant AI system's non-compliance is not restricted to national territory:** The market surveillance authority must inform the Commission and the other Member States without undue delay of:
  * The results of its evaluation [Art. 79(3)];
  * The actions it has required the operator to take [Art. 79(3)].

* **When other Member States respond to a procedure concerning a non-compliant AI system:** Other market surveillance authorities must inform the Commission and other Member States without undue delay of:
  * Any measures adopted [Art. 79(7)];
  * Any additional information at their disposal relating to the non-compliance [Art. 79(7)];
  * Their objections, in the event of disagreement with the notified national measure [Art. 79(7)].

* **When risks to fundamental rights are identified during an evaluation:** The market surveillance authority must inform and fully cooperate with the relevant national public authorities or bodies referred to in Article 77(1) [Art. 79(2)].

* **When non-compliance is found following an evaluation:** The market surveillance authority must also inform the relevant notified body [Art. 79(2)].

Under Annex X, point 1, of the AI Act, the Union legislative acts covering the Schengen Information System are:

* **Regulation (EU) 2018/1860** on the use of the Schengen Information System for the return of illegally staying third-country nationals [Annex X, point 1];
* **Regulation (EU) 2018/1861** on the establishment, operation and use of the Schengen Information System (SIS) in the field of border checks [Annex X, point 1];
* **Regulation (EU) 2018/1862** on the establishment, operation and use of the Schengen Information System (SIS) in the field of police cooperation and judicial cooperation in criminal matters [Annex X, point 1].

Under Article 112(10), when submitting appropriate proposals to amend this Regulation, the Commission shall in particular take into account [Art. 112(10)]:

* developments in technology [Art. 112(10)];
* the effect of AI systems on health and safety [Art. 112(10)];
* the effect of AI systems on fundamental rights [Art. 112(10)];
* the state of progress in the information society [Art. 112(10)].

Under Article 89(2), downstream providers have the right to lodge a complaint alleging an infringement of the Regulation [Art. 89(2)]. The complaint must be duly reasoned and indicate at least:

* The point of contact of the provider of the general-purpose AI model concerned [Art. 89(2)(a)];
* A description of the relevant facts, the provisions of the Regulation concerned, and the reason why the downstream provider considers that the provider of the general-purpose AI model concerned infringed the Regulation [Art. 89(2)(b)];
* Any other information that the downstream provider considers relevant, including, where appropriate, information gathered on its own initiative [Art. 89(2)(c)].

Yes, providers or prospective providers are required to register the testing in real world conditions before conducting it outside AI regulatory sandboxes [Art. 60(4)(c)]. 

Depending on the type of high-risk AI system listed in Annex III, registration must be carried out as follows:

* **Standard Annex III systems:** The provider or prospective provider must register the testing in real world conditions in accordance with Article 71(4) with a Union-wide unique single identification number and with the information specified in Annex IX [Art. 60(4)(c)].
* **Law enforcement, migration, asylum, and border control systems (points 1, 6, and 7 of Annex III):** The provider or prospective provider must register the testing in the "secure non-public section of the EU database according to Article 49(4), point (d), with a Union-wide unique single identification number and with the information specified therein" [Art. 60(4)(c)].
* **Critical infrastructure systems (point 2 of Annex III):** The provider or prospective provider must register the testing in real world conditions in accordance with Article 49(5) [Art. 60(4)(c)].

When registering under Annex IX, the information submitted must include:
* A Union-wide unique single identification number of the testing [Annex IX, point 1];
* Name and contact details of the provider/prospective provider and deployers involved [Annex IX, point 2];
* A brief description of the AI system, its intended purpose, and identification information [Annex IX, point 3];
* A summary of the main characteristics of the real-world testing plan [Annex IX, point 4];
* Information on the suspension or termination of the testing [Annex IX, point 5].

An authorised representative must keep the documentation for a high-risk AI system for a period of **10 years** after the system has been placed on the market or put into service [Art. 22(3)(b)].

Specifically, their mandate empowers them to keep at the disposal of competent authorities and relevant national authorities or bodies:
* the contact details of the provider that appointed them [Art. 22(3)(b)];
* a copy of the EU declaration of conformity referred to in Article 47 [Art. 22(3)(b)];
* the technical documentation [Art. 22(3)(b)]; and
* if applicable, the certificate issued by the notified body [Art. 22(3)(b)].

Based on the provided excerpts:

**Who sets up the scientific panel:**
The Commission establishes the scientific panel of independent experts by means of an implementing act adopted in accordance with the examination procedure referred to in Article 98(2) [Art. 68(1)]. The experts are selected by the Commission, which also determines their number in consultation with the Board [Art. 68(2)].

**Purpose of the scientific panel:**
The scientific panel is established to "support the enforcement activities under this Regulation" [Art. 68(1)]. Specifically, it advises and supports the AI Office and other authorities in the following tasks [Art. 68(3)]:
* Supporting the implementation and enforcement of the Regulation regarding general-purpose AI models and systems, which includes:
  * Alerting the AI Office of possible systemic risks at Union level under Article 90 [Art. 68(3)(a)(i)], including issuing qualified alerts where it suspects a model poses a concrete identifiable risk at Union level or meets the conditions in Article 51 [Art. 90(1)];
  * Contributing to developing tools and methodologies (including benchmarks) for evaluating model and system capabilities [Art. 68(3)(a)(ii)];
  * Providing advice on classifying general-purpose AI models with systemic risk and classifying various general-purpose AI models and systems [Art. 68(3)(a)(iii), (iv)];
  * Contributing to the development of tools and templates [Art. 68(3)(a)(v)];
* Supporting the work of market surveillance authorities at their request [Art. 68(3)(b)];
* Supporting cross-border market surveillance activities under Article 74(11) [Art. 68(3)(c)];
* Supporting the AI Office in its duties regarding the Union safeguard procedure under Article 81 [Art. 68(3)(d)].

Under the EU AI Act, the role of the scientific panel of independent experts is to advise and support the AI Office [Art. 68(3)]. 

Its duties and role in relation to the AI Office include:

* **Supporting implementation and enforcement regarding general-purpose AI models and systems** [Art. 68(3)(a)], in particular by:
  * Alerting the AI Office of possible systemic risks at Union level of general-purpose AI models under Article 90 [Art. 68(3)(a)(i)], specifically by providing a "qualified alert" when it suspects a model poses a concrete identifiable risk at Union level or meets the conditions in Article 51 [Art. 90(1)]. Such an alert allows the Commission, through the AI Office, to exercise supervisory and enforcement powers to assess the matter [Art. 90(2)];
  * Contributing to the development of tools and methodologies for evaluating capabilities of general-purpose AI models and systems, including through benchmarks [Art. 68(3)(a)(ii)];
  * Providing advice on the classification of general-purpose AI models with systemic risk [Art. 68(3)(a)(iii)];
  * Providing advice on the classification of various general-purpose AI models and systems [Art. 68(3)(a)(iv)];
  * Contributing to the development of tools and templates [Art. 68(3)(a)(v)].
* **Supporting safeguard procedures**: Supporting the AI Office in carrying out its duties in the context of the Union safeguard procedure pursuant to Article 81 [Art. 68(3)(d)].
* **Requesting assistance and information**: Requesting the assistance of the AI Office for the performance of its tasks [Art. 68(5)], and submitting duly substantiated requests to the Commission to issue requests for information to providers of general-purpose AI models when necessary and proportionate for fulfilling its tasks [Art. 91(3)].
* **Operational independence and conflict management**: The experts must perform their tasks with impartiality, objectivity, and confidentiality, and neither seek nor take instructions from anyone [Art. 68(4)]. In turn, the AI Office must establish systems and procedures to actively manage and prevent potential conflicts of interest concerning the panel's experts [Art. 68(4)]. 

*(Note: References in the Regulation to the AI Office are construed as references to the Commission [Art. 3(47)]).*

Under the EU AI Act, a qualified alert from the scientific panel must be "duly reasoned" and indicate at least the following information [Art. 90(3)]:

* The point of contact of the provider of the general-purpose AI model with systemic risk concerned [Art. 90(3)(a)];
* A description of the relevant facts and the reasons for the alert by the scientific panel [Art. 90(3)(b)];
* Any other information that the scientific panel considers to be relevant, including, where appropriate, information gathered on its own initiative [Art. 90(3)(c)].

When deciding on the amount of an administrative fine, authorities must consider all relevant circumstances of the specific situation, taking into account specific factors depending on the entity involved:

**1. General operators (under Article 99):**
Authorities must, as appropriate, give regard to [Art. 99(7)]:
* The nature, gravity, and duration of the infringement and its consequences, considering the purpose of the AI system, and where appropriate, the number of affected persons and the level of damage suffered [Art. 99(7)(a)];
* Whether administrative fines have already been applied to the same operator for the same infringement by other market surveillance authorities [Art. 99(7)(b)];
* Whether administrative fines have already been applied by other authorities for infringements of other Union or national law resulting from the same activity or omission [Art. 99(7)(c)];
* The operator's size, annual turnover, and market share [Art. 99(7)(d)];
* Any other aggravating or mitigating factors, such as financial benefits gained or losses avoided directly or indirectly [Art. 99(7)(e)];
* The degree of cooperation with national competent authorities to remedy the infringement and mitigate adverse effects [Art. 99(7)(f)];
* The operator's degree of responsibility, considering implemented technical and organisational measures [Art. 99(7)(g)];
* The manner in which the infringement became known, particularly whether and to what extent the operator notified it [Art. 99(7)(h)];
* The intentional or negligent character of the infringement [Art. 99(7)(i)];
* Any action taken to mitigate harm suffered by affected persons [Art. 99(7)(j)].

**2. Union institutions, bodies, offices, and agencies (under Article 100):**
The European Data Protection Supervisor gives due regard to [Art. 100(1)]:
* The nature, gravity, and duration of the infringement and its consequences, the AI system's purpose, and where appropriate, the number of affected persons and damage suffered [Art. 100(1)(a)];
* The degree of responsibility, taking into account implemented technical and organisational measures [Art. 100(1)(b)];
* Actions taken to mitigate damage suffered by affected persons [Art. 100(1)(c)];
* The degree of cooperation with the European Data Protection Supervisor, including compliance with previously ordered measures regarding the same subject matter [Art. 100(1)(d)];
* Any similar previous infringements [Art. 100(1)(e)];
* How the infringement became known, particularly whether and to what extent it was notified [Art. 100(1)(f)];
* The annual budget of the institution, body, office, or agency [Art. 100(1)(g)].

**3. Providers of general-purpose AI models (under Article 101):**
When fixing the amount of a fine or periodic penalty payment, the Commission considers [Art. 101(1)]:
* The nature, gravity, and duration of the infringement [Art. 101(1)];
* Due account of the principles of proportionality and appropriateness [Art. 101(1)];
* Commitments made in accordance with Article 93(3) or in relevant codes of practice under Article 56 [Art. 101(1)];
* Ensuring the fines are effective, proportionate, and dissuasive [Art. 101(3)].

Under Annex IX, the following information must be provided and thereafter kept up to date when registering testing in real world conditions for high-risk AI systems in accordance with Article 60 [Annex IX]:

* A Union-wide unique single identification number of the testing in real world conditions [Annex IX, point 1];
* The name and contact details of the provider or prospective provider and of the deployers involved in the testing in real world conditions [Annex IX, point 2];
* A brief description of the AI system, its intended purpose, and other information necessary for the identification of the system [Annex IX, point 3];
* A summary of the main characteristics of the plan for testing in real world conditions [Annex IX, point 4];
* Information on the suspension or termination of the testing in real world conditions [Annex IX, point 5].

This information applies to the registration required under Article 60(4)(c) [Art. 60(4)(c)]. Please note that this is not legal advice.

Under Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6, providers must provide and keep up to date the following information regarding AI systems to be registered in accordance with Article 49(2) [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6]:

1. The name, address and contact details of the provider [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6];
2. Where the submission of information is carried out by another person on behalf of the provider, the name, address and contact details of that person [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6];
3. The name, address and contact details of the authorised representative, where applicable [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6];
4. The AI system trade name and any additional unambiguous reference allowing the identification and traceability of the AI system [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6];
5. A description of the intended purpose of the AI system [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6];
6. The condition or conditions under Article 6(3) based on which the AI system is considered to be not-high-risk [Annex VIII, Section B, points 1–6].

This is not legal advice.

Based on the provided excerpts, a provider established in a third country wishing to make a high-risk AI system available or place it on the EU market must fulfill the following obligations and steps:

* **Appoint an authorised representative:** Prior to making their high-risk AI systems available on the Union market, providers established in third countries must appoint, by written mandate, an authorised representative established in the Union [Art. 22(1)]. The mandate must empower the authorised representative to carry out specified tasks, including, where applicable, complying with the registration obligations referred to in Article 49(1) or ensuring that the information referred to in point 3 of Section A of Annex VIII is correct if registration is done by the provider [Art. 22(3)(e)].
* **Ensure compliance with technical requirements:** The provider must ensure the high-risk AI system complies with the requirements set out in Section 2 [Art. 16(a)] and with accessibility requirements in accordance with Directives (EU) 2016/2102 and (EU) 2019/882 [Art. 16(l)].
* **Implement management and documentation systems:** The provider must:
  * Have a quality management system in place that complies with Article 17 [Art. 16(c)];
  * Draw up technical documentation in accordance with Article 11 and Annex IV [Art. 23(1)(b)];
  * Keep the documentation referred to in Article 18 [Art. 16(d)];
  * Keep automatically generated logs when under their control as referred to in Article 19 [Art. 16(e)].
* **Carry out conformity assessment and CE marking:** 
  * Ensure the system undergoes the relevant conformity assessment procedure referred to in Article 43 prior to being placed on the market or put into service [Art. 16(f)], [Art. 23(1)(a)];
  * Draw up an EU declaration of conformity in accordance with Article 47 [Art. 16(g)], [Art. 23(1)(c)];
  * Affix the CE marking to the system or, where not possible, on its packaging or accompanying documentation in accordance with Article 48 [Art. 16(h)], [Art. 23(1)(c)];
  * Accompany the system with instructions for use [Art. 23(1)(c)].
* **Identification and registration:**
  * Indicate their name, registered trade name or trade mark, and contact address on the system, packaging, or accompanying documentation [Art. 16(b)];
  * Comply with the registration obligations referred to in Article 49(1) [Art. 16(i)].
* **Ongoing compliance and authority requests:**
  * Take necessary corrective actions and provide information as required in Article 20 [Art. 16(j)];
  * Demonstrate conformity with Section 2 requirements upon a reasoned request from a national competent authority [Art. 16(k)].

*(Note: High-risk AI systems placed on the market will also be subject to verifications by importers to ensure these provider steps have been completed before the system is placed on the market [Art. 23(1)].)*

This is not legal advice.

Yes, the European Commission is required to develop practical guidance, and it must specifically take into account the needs of small businesses.

**Practical Implementation Guidelines**
* The Commission must develop guidelines on the practical implementation of the Regulation [Art. 96(1)]. In particular, these guidelines will cover:
  * The application of requirements and obligations in Articles 8 to 15 and Article 25 [Art. 96(1)(a)];
  * Prohibited practices under Article 5 [Art. 96(1)(b)];
  * Substantial modification provisions [Art. 96(1)(c)];
  * Transparency obligations under Article 50 [Art. 96(1)(d)];
  * The relationship with Union harmonisation legislation in Annex I and other Union law [Art. 96(1)(e)];
  * The application of the definition of an AI system under Article 3, point (1) [Art. 96(1)(f)].
* By 2 February 2026, after consulting the European Artificial Intelligence Board, the Commission must provide guidelines specifying the practical implementation of Article 6 (classification of high-risk AI systems) alongside a comprehensive list of practical examples of high-risk and non-high-risk use cases [Art. 6(5)]. For deadlines, later amendments to the Act are not reflected in this text.

**Consideration of Small Businesses**
* When issuing its implementation guidelines, the Commission is explicitly required to *"pay particular attention to the needs of SMEs including start-ups, of local public authorities and of the sectors most likely to be affected by this Regulation"* [Art. 96(1)].
* The Commission must also develop guidelines on the specific elements of the quality management system that microenterprises may fulfill in a simplified manner [Art. 63(1)], [Recital 146]. 
* Additionally, to allow SMEs (including start-ups) to provide required technical documentation in a simplified manner, the Commission must establish a simplified technical documentation form targeted at the needs of small and microenterprises [Art. 11(1)].

Before placing a high-risk AI system on the market, importers must ensure that the system is in conformity with the Regulation by verifying that:

* The relevant conformity assessment procedure referred to in Article 43 has been carried out by the provider [Art. 23(1)(a)];
* The provider has drawn up the technical documentation in accordance with Article 11 and Annex IV [Art. 23(1)(b)];
* The system bears the required CE marking and is accompanied by the EU declaration of conformity referred to in Article 47 and instructions for use [Art. 23(1)(c)];
* The provider has appointed an authorised representative in accordance with Article 22(1) [Art. 23(1)(d)].

Additionally, importers must check that the system is not non-compliant, falsified, or accompanied by falsified documentation; if an importer has sufficient reason to consider that any of these apply, it must not place the system on the market until it is brought into conformity [Art. 23(2)]. If the system presents a risk within the meaning of Article 79(1), the importer must also inform the provider, the authorised representative, and the market surveillance authorities [Art. 23(2)].

Under Annex IV, point 1, a general description of the AI system must include the following elements:

* Its intended purpose, the name of the provider, and the version of the system reflecting its relation to previous versions [Annex IV, point 1(a)];
* How the AI system interacts with, or can be used to interact with, hardware or software, including other AI systems that are not part of the AI system itself, where applicable [Annex IV, point 1(b)];
* The versions of relevant software or firmware, and any requirements related to version updates [Annex IV, point 1(c)];
* A description of all the forms in which the AI system is placed on the market or put into service (e.g., software packages embedded into hardware, downloads, or APIs) [Annex IV, point 1(d)];
* A description of the hardware on which the AI system is intended to run [Annex IV, point 1(e)];
* Where the AI system is a component of products, photographs or illustrations showing external features, the marking, and internal layout of those products [Annex IV, point 1(f)];
* A basic description of the user-interface provided to the deployer [Annex IV, point 1(g)];
* Instructions for use for the deployer, and a basic description of the user-interface provided to the deployer, where applicable [Annex IV, point 1(h)].

When assessing whether an AI system poses a risk equivalent to, or greater than, existing high-risk AI systems in order to amend Annex III [Art. 7(1)], the Commission considers:

* "the extent to which the use of an AI system has already caused harm to health and safety, has had an adverse impact on fundamental rights or has given rise to significant concerns in relation to the likelihood of such harm or adverse impact" [Art. 7(2)(e)];
* whether this is demonstrated, for instance, "by reports or documented allegations submitted to national competent authorities or by other reports, as appropriate" [Art. 7(2)(e)].

Under Article 93(1), where necessary and appropriate, the Commission may request providers to take the following actions:

* Take appropriate measures to comply with the obligations set out in Articles 53 and 54 [Art. 93(1)];
* Implement mitigation measures, where the evaluation carried out in accordance with Article 92 has given rise to serious and substantiated concern of a systemic risk at Union level [Art. 93(1)];
* Restrict the making available on the market, withdraw, or recall the model [Art. 93(1)].

Under the EU AI Act, high-risk AI systems that use techniques involving the training of AI models must be developed using training, validation, and testing data sets that satisfy specific quality criteria whenever such data sets are used [Art. 10(1)]. If the system does not use training techniques, these requirements apply only to the testing data sets [Art. 10(6)]. 

The requirements include:

* **Data Governance and Management Practices:** Training, validation, and testing data sets must undergo practices appropriate for the intended purpose, concerning in particular [Art. 10(2)]:
  * relevant design choices [Art. 10(2)(a)];
  * data collection processes and origin, including the original purpose if personal data is involved [Art. 10(2)(b)];
  * data-preparation operations (such as annotation, labelling, cleaning, updating, enrichment, and aggregation) [Art. 10(2)(c)];
  * formulating assumptions regarding what the data measures and represents [Art. 10(2)(d)];
  * assessing the availability, quantity, and suitability of needed data sets [Art. 10(2)(e)];
  * examining possible biases likely to affect health and safety, negatively impact fundamental rights, or lead to prohibited discrimination under Union law (especially where data outputs influence future inputs), and applying appropriate measures to detect, prevent, and mitigate those biases [Art. 10(2)(f)], [Art. 10(2)(g)];
  * identifying and addressing data gaps or shortcomings that prevent compliance [Art. 10(2)(h)].

* **Data Set Characteristics:** Data sets must be relevant, sufficiently representative, and "to the best extent possible, free of errors and complete in view of the intended purpose" [Art. 10(3)]. They must have appropriate statistical properties—including regarding targeted persons or groups—either individually or combined [Art. 10(3)]. They must also account for specific geographical, contextual, behavioural, or functional settings of the intended purpose [Art. 10(4)].

* **Processing Special Categories of Personal Data:** Providers may exceptionally process special categories of personal data strictly to detect and correct biases if other data (like synthetic or anonymised data) cannot effectively fulfill the task, provided strict safeguards are met (e.g., pseudonymisation, access restrictions, prohibition on third-party access, timely deletion, and documentation in processing records) [Art. 10(5)].

* **Quality Management System:** Providers must document systems and procedures for data management in a quality management system, covering data acquisition, collection, analysis, labelling, storage, filtration, mining, aggregation, retention, and any other pre-market operations [Art. 17(1)(f)].

Under the AI Act, an ‘authorised representative’ is:

* A natural or legal person located or established in the Union [Art. 3(5)];
* Who has received and accepted a written mandate from a provider of an AI system or a general-purpose AI model [Art. 3(5)];
* To perform and carry out on that provider's behalf the obligations and procedures established by the Regulation [Art. 3(5)].

Under the AI Act, a ‘publicly accessible space’ means:

* Any publicly or privately owned physical place accessible to an undetermined number of natural persons [Art. 3(44)];
* This applies regardless of whether certain conditions for access may apply [Art. 3(44)];
* It also applies regardless of potential capacity restrictions [Art. 3(44)].

Under the AI Act, a ‘serious incident’ means an incident or malfunctioning of an AI system that directly or indirectly leads to any of the following [Art. 3(49)]:

* the death of a person, or serious harm to a person’s health [Art. 3(49)(a)];
* a serious and irreversible disruption of the management or operation of critical infrastructure [Art. 3(49)(b)];
* the infringement of obligations under Union law intended to protect fundamental rights [Art. 3(49)(c)]; or
* serious harm to property or the environment [Art. 3(49)(d)].

Under the provided excerpts of the EU AI Act, using an AI system to recruit, select, filter job applications, or evaluate candidates classifies the system as high-risk [Annex III, point 4(a)], [Recital 57]. 

To use such a system properly, the provided excerpts set out the following requirements and criteria:

* **Inform workers and their representatives:** If you are an employer putting into service or using a high-risk AI system at the workplace, you must inform affected workers and workers' representatives beforehand that they will be subject to the system's use, following applicable Union and national rules and procedures [Art. 26(7)].
* **Enable human oversight:** The system must be provided to you so that assigned individuals are enabled to exercise proper human oversight [Art. 14(4)]. This includes enabling them to:
  * Properly understand the system's capacities and limitations, monitor its operation, and detect/address anomalies or unexpected performance [Art. 14(4)(a)];
  * Remain aware of automation bias (the tendency to automatically rely or over-rely on the system's output), particularly when it provides recommendations for human decisions [Art. 14(4)(b)];
  * Correctly interpret the system’s output [Art. 14(4)(c)];
  * Decide not to use the system, or disregard, override, or reverse its output in any situation [Art. 14(4)(d)];
  * Intervene or stop the system safely via a "stop" button or similar procedure [Art. 14(4)(e)].
* **Registration information (if applicable under Article 49(3)):** If required to register the system in the EU database, deployers must provide and update specific details, including deployer contact info, the URL of the provider’s database entry, a summary of the fundamental rights impact assessment, and a summary of any applicable data protection impact assessment [Annex VIII, Section C, points 1–5].
* **Traceability and logging:** High-risk systems must feature logging capabilities enabling the recording of events to identify potential risks or substantial modifications, facilitate post-market monitoring, and monitor operations [Art. 12(2)].

*(Note: The provided excerpts do not contain the full set of deployer obligations under Article 26 or the specific conditions of Article 49(3) and Article 27.)*

This explanation outlines the Act's criteria based on the provided text and is not legal advice.

No, not just any team member can monitor or oversee a high-risk AI system. The AI Act sets specific criteria for the individuals assigned to these tasks:

* **General human oversight (deployers):** Deployers must assign human oversight specifically to natural persons who have the "necessary competence, training and authority, as well as the necessary support" [Art. 26(2)]. 
* **Operational capabilities:** The individuals assigned to oversee the system must be enabled to:
  * Properly understand the system's capacities and limitations and duly monitor its operation (detecting anomalies, dysfunctions, and unexpected performance) [Art. 14(4)(a)];
  * Remain aware of automation bias (the tendency to automatically rely or over-rely on system output) [Art. 14(4)(b)];
  * Correctly interpret the system's output [Art. 14(4)(c)];
  * Decide to disregard, override, reverse, or not use the system [Art. 14(4)(d)];
  * Intervene or halt the system safely via a "stop" button or similar procedure [Art. 14(4)(e)].
* **Monitoring obligations:** Deployers must monitor the AI system's operation specifically "on the basis of the instructions for use" [Art. 26(5)].
* **Real-world testing oversight (if applicable):** If testing in real-world conditions outside sandboxes, oversight must be conducted by individuals who are "suitably qualified in the relevant field and have the necessary capacity, training and authority to perform their tasks" [Art. 60(4)(j)].

This explanation is based on the statutory criteria and does not constitute legal advice.

Yes, under Article 6(2), AI systems considered high-risk include those listed in specific areas set out in Annex III [Annex III], [Art. 6(2)]. 

The provided excerpts identify the following specific areas:
* **Biometrics**, in so far as their use is permitted under relevant Union or national law [Annex III, point 1];
* **Access to and enjoyment of essential private services and essential public services and benefits** [Annex III, point 5];
* An area listed in **point 2 of Annex III** is also referenced [Art. 27(1)].

Furthermore, the Commission may adopt delegated acts to amend Annex III by adding or modifying use-cases, provided that the AI systems are intended to be used in any of the areas listed in Annex III [Art. 7(1)(a)].

Under the EU AI Act, providers of high-risk AI systems must implement specific procedures, governance practices, and documentation regarding data collection and handling:

* **Quality Management System (QMS) Procedures:** Providers must establish a quality management system documented in written policies, procedures, and instructions [Art. 17(1)]. This must include:
  * "systems and procedures for data management, including data acquisition, data collection, data analysis, data labelling, data storage, data filtration, data mining, data aggregation, data retention and any other operation regarding the data" carried out before and for placing the system on the market or putting it into service [Art. 17(1)(f)].
  * "systems and procedures for record-keeping of all relevant documentation and information" [Art. 17(1)(k)].

* **Data Governance and Management Practices:** Whenever high-risk AI systems use techniques involving training models with data, the training, validation, and testing data sets must undergo data governance and management practices appropriate for their intended purpose [Art. 10(1), Art. 10(2)]. These practices must specifically concern:
  * Relevant design choices [Art. 10(2)(a)];
  * "data collection processes and the origin of data, and in the case of personal data, the original purpose of the data collection" [Art. 10(2)(b)];
  * Data-preparation processing operations (such as annotation, labelling, cleaning, updating, enrichment, and aggregation) [Art. 10(2)(c)];
  * Formulation of assumptions regarding what data are supposed to measure and represent [Art. 10(2)(d)];
  * Assessment of data availability, quantity, and suitability [Art. 10(2)(e)];
  * Examination for biases that may affect health, safety, or fundamental rights, or lead to unlawful discrimination, along with appropriate measures to detect, prevent, and mitigate those biases [Art. 10(2)(f), Art. 10(2)(g)];
  * Identification and addressing of data gaps or shortcomings [Art. 10(2)(h)];
  * Taking into account the particular characteristics of the specific geographical, contextual, behavioural, or functional setting of intended use [Art. 10(4)].

* **Technical and QMS Documentation Keeping:** 
  * Providers must draw up technical documentation demonstrating compliance before the system is placed on the market or put into service, containing at least the elements set out in Annex IV, and keep it up to date [Art. 11(1)]. SMEs and start-ups may provide these elements in a simplified manner using a form established by the Commission [Art. 11(1)].
  * Providers are obligated to keep this technical documentation and the QMS documentation at the disposal of national competent authorities for a period ending 10 years after the system is placed on the market or put into service [Art. 16(c), Art. 16(d), Art. 18(1)(a), Art. 18(1)(b)].

According to the provided excerpts, the participants in the standardisation process must seek to enhance multi-stakeholder governance to ensure:

* A balanced representation of interests [Art. 40(3)];
* The effective participation of all relevant stakeholders in accordance with Articles 5, 6, and 7 of Regulation (EU) No 1025/2012 [Art. 40(3)].

Under the AI Act, **‘informed consent’** means a subject’s freely given, specific, unambiguous, and voluntary expression of their willingness to participate in a particular testing in real-world conditions, after having been informed of all aspects of the testing relevant to their decision to participate [Art. 3(59)].

For testing outside AI regulatory sandboxes, the key requirements and details regarding informed consent include:

* **Timing and Information Provided:** Freely-given informed consent must be obtained prior to participation, following the provision of concise, clear, relevant, and understandable information regarding [Art. 61(1)]:
  * The nature, objectives, and possible inconvenience linked to participation [Art. 61(1)(a)];
  * The conditions and expected duration of participation [Art. 61(1)(b)];
  * Their rights and guarantees, specifically the right to refuse or withdraw at any time without detriment or justification [Art. 61(1)(c)];
  * Arrangements to request the reversal or disregarding of the AI system's predictions, recommendations, or decisions [Art. 61(1)(d)];
  * The Union-wide unique single identification number of the testing and the contact details of the provider or its legal representative [Art. 61(1)(e)].
* **Form and Documentation:** The consent must be dated and documented, and a copy must be provided to the subjects or their legal representative [Art. 61(2)].
* **Withdrawal:** Subjects or their legally designated representative may revoke consent at any time without detriment or justification, and may request the immediate and permanent deletion of their personal data (withdrawal does not affect activities already carried out) [Art. 60(5)].
* **Law Enforcement Exception:** In the case of law enforcement, where seeking informed consent would prevent the AI system from being tested, testing may occur without it provided that the testing and its outcome have no negative effect on the subjects, and their personal data are deleted after the test [Art. 60(4)(i)].

When deciding whether to impose an administrative fine and when determining its amount, the factors taken into account depend on the entity involved:

**1. For general operators and infringements under Article 99**, all relevant circumstances of the specific situation are considered, giving regard as appropriate to [Art. 99(7)]:
* The nature, gravity, and duration of the infringement and its consequences, considering the purpose of the AI system, and where appropriate, the number of affected persons and the level of damage suffered [Art. 99(7)(a)];
* Whether administrative fines have already been applied by other market surveillance authorities to the same operator for the same infringement [Art. 99(7)(b)];
* Whether administrative fines have already been applied by other authorities to the same operator for infringements of other Union or national law resulting from the same activity or omission [Art. 99(7)(c)];
* The operator's size, annual turnover, and market share [Art. 99(7)(d)];
* Any other aggravating or mitigating factor, such as financial benefits gained or losses avoided (directly or indirectly) from the infringement [Art. 99(7)(e)];
* The degree of cooperation with national competent authorities to remedy the infringement and mitigate possible adverse effects [Art. 99(7)(f)];
* The operator's degree of responsibility, taking into account the technical and organisational measures implemented [Art. 99(7)(g)];
* The manner in which the infringement became known to authorities, particularly whether and to what extent the operator notified it [Art. 99(7)(h)];
* The intentional or negligent character of the infringement [Art. 99(7)(i)];
* Any action taken by the operator to mitigate the harm suffered by affected persons [Art. 99(7)(j)].

**2. For Union institutions, bodies, offices, and agencies**, the European Data Protection Supervisor takes into account all relevant circumstances, giving due regard to [Art. 100(1)]:
* The nature, gravity, and duration of the infringement and its consequences, taking into account the purpose of the AI system concerned, the number of affected persons, and the level of damage suffered [Art. 100(1)(a)];
* The degree of responsibility, considering technical and organisational measures implemented [Art. 100(1)(b)];
* Any action taken to mitigate damage suffered by affected persons [Art. 100(1)(c)];
* The degree of cooperation with the European Data Protection Supervisor to remedy the infringement and mitigate adverse effects, including compliance with previously ordered measures regarding the same subject matter [Art. 100(1)(d)];
* Any similar previous infringements [Art. 100(1)(e)];
* How the infringement became known, particularly whether and to what extent it was notified [Art. 100(1)(f)];
* The annual budget of the Union institution, body, office, or agency [Art. 100(1)(g)].

**3. For providers of general-purpose AI models**, the Commission considers:
* The "nature, gravity and duration of the infringement, taking due account of the principles of proportionality and appropriateness" [Art. 101(1)];
* Commitments made pursuant to Article 93(3) or made in relevant codes of practice in accordance with Article 56 [Art. 101(1)].

For a provider that is a financial institution subject to requirements regarding internal governance, arrangements, or processes under Union financial services law, the EU AI Act specifies the following record retention obligations:

* **General documentation retention period:** For a period ending 10 years after the high-risk AI system has been placed on the market or put into service, the provider must keep at the disposal of national competent authorities [Art. 18(1)]:
  * The technical documentation referred to in Article 11 [Art. 18(1)(a)];
  * The documentation concerning the quality management system referred to in Article 17 [Art. 18(1)(b)];
  * Documentation concerning changes approved by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(c)];
  * Decisions and other documents issued by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(d)];
  * The EU declaration of conformity referred to in Article 47 [Art. 18(1)(e)], which must also be kept for 10 years after placement on the market or putting into service and provided upon request [Art. 47(1)].

* **Integration with financial services law documentation:**
  * **Technical documentation:** Providers that are financial institutions must maintain the technical documentation "as part of the documentation kept under the relevant Union financial services law" [Art. 18(3)].
  * **Automatically generated logs:** Providers that are financial institutions must maintain the logs automatically generated by their high-risk AI systems "as part of the documentation kept under the relevant financial services law" [Art. 19(2)].

* **Quality management system record-keeping:** While providers must include "systems and procedures for record-keeping of all relevant documentation and information" in their quality management system [Art. 17(1)(k)], for financial institutions this obligation (along with the rest of paragraph 1, excluding points (g), (h), and (i)) is "deemed to be fulfilled by complying with the rules on internal governance arrangements or processes pursuant to the relevant Union financial services law" [Art. 17(4)].

The main purpose and objective of the EU AI Act is to:

* Improve the functioning of the internal market [Art. 1(1)], [Recital 1], [Recital 176];
* Promote the uptake of human-centric and trustworthy artificial intelligence (AI) [Art. 1(1)], [Recital 1], [Recital 176];
* Ensure a high level of protection of health, safety, and fundamental rights enshrined in the Charter, including democracy, the rule of law, and environmental protection, against the harmful effects of AI systems in the Union [Art. 1(1)], [Recital 1], [Recital 176]; and
* Support innovation [Art. 1(1)], [Recital 1], [Recital 176].

Additionally, the rules aim to ensure the smooth functioning of the internal market and the cross-border free movement of AI-based goods and services [Recital 1], [Recital 8], support the European human-centric approach to AI, and establish the Union as a global leader in secure, trustworthy, and ethical AI [Recital 8].

Under the EU AI Act, the logging capabilities of high-risk AI systems must enable the recording of events relevant for:

* "identifying situations that may result in the high-risk AI system presenting a risk within the meaning of Article 79(1) or in a substantial modification" [Art. 12(2)(a)];
* "facilitating the post-market monitoring referred to in Article 72" [Art. 12(2)(b)]; and
* "monitoring the operation of high-risk AI systems referred to in Article 26(5)" [Art. 12(2)(c)].

Additionally, for high-risk AI systems referred to in point 1(a) of Annex III, the logging capabilities must provide, at a minimum, the recording of:
* the period of each use of the system (start date and time, and end date and time of each use) [Art. 12(3)(a)];
* the reference database against which input data has been checked by the system [Art. 12(3)(b)];
* the input data for which the search has led to a match [Art. 12(3)(c)]; and
* the identification of the natural persons involved in verifying the results under Article 14(5) [Art. 12(3)(d)].

The Commission decides to classify and include a general-purpose AI model on the list of models with systemic risk based on the following rules and procedures [Art. 51(1)], [Art. 52(6)]:

* **Models with high-impact capabilities:** A model is classified as having systemic risk if it has high-impact capabilities evaluated using appropriate technical tools and methodologies, including indicators and benchmarks [Art. 51(1)(a)].
  * Providers must notify the Commission without delay (and within two weeks) when their model meets or is known to meet this condition [Art. 52(1)].
  * If the Commission becomes aware of a model presenting systemic risks that has not been notified, it may decide to designate it as a model with systemic risk [Art. 52(1)].
  * If a provider submits arguments pursuant to paragraph 2 that the model does not present systemic risks, but the Commission concludes those arguments are not sufficiently substantiated, the Commission rejects the arguments, and the model is considered to be a model with systemic risk [Art. 52(3)].

* **Commission designation based on equivalent impact and Annex XIII criteria:** The Commission may decide—either *ex officio* or following a qualified alert from the scientific panel—to designate a model as presenting systemic risks if it has capabilities or an impact equivalent to high-impact capabilities [Art. 51(1)(b)], [Art. 52(4)]. To determine this, the Commission must take into account the criteria set out in Annex XIII [Art. 51(1)(b)], [Art. 52(4)], [Annex XIII]:
  * Number of parameters [Annex XIII(a)];
  * Quality or size of the dataset (e.g., measured through tokens) [Annex XIII(b)];
  * Amount of computation used for training (measured in FLOPs or indicated by variables like estimated cost, time, or energy consumption) [Annex XIII(c)];
  * Input and output modalities (e.g., large language models, text-to-image, multi-modality), state-of-the-art thresholds for each modality, and specific input/output types [Annex XIII(d)];
  * Benchmarks and capability evaluations (e.g., tasks performed without additional training, adaptability, autonomy, scalability, and tools accessed) [Annex XIII(e)];
  * Internal market reach and impact, which is presumed if made available to at least 10,000 registered business users established in the Union [Annex XIII(f)];
  * Number of registered end-users [Annex XIII(g)].

* **Reassessment:** A designated provider may request a reassessment with new, objective, and detailed reasons at the earliest six months after the designation decision (or six months after a decision to maintain designation), upon which the Commission may decide to reassess whether the model still presents systemic risks under Annex XIII [Art. 52(5)].

The Commission publishes and keeps updated the resulting list of general-purpose AI models with systemic risk, subject to the protection of intellectual property, confidential business information, or trade secrets [Art. 52(6)].

Under the provided excerpts of the EU AI Act, market surveillance authorities are triggered to take measures against an AI system in the following situations:

* **Presentation of a risk:** When an authority has sufficient reason to consider that an AI system presents a risk (giving particular attention to vulnerable groups and risks to fundamental rights) [Art. 79(2)], or finds that even though a high-risk AI system complies with the Regulation, it "nevertheless presents a risk to the health or safety of persons, to fundamental rights, or to other aspects of public interest protection" [Art. 82(1)].
* **Non-compliance with requirements and obligations:** When an evaluation reveals that the AI system fails to comply with the requirements and obligations laid down in the Regulation [Art. 79(2)]. Specific grounds for non-compliance noted when taking provisional measures include:
  * Non-compliance with prohibited AI practices referred to in Article 5 [Art. 79(6)(a)];
  * A failure of a high-risk AI system to meet the requirements set out in Chapter III, Section 2 [Art. 79(6)(b)];
  * Shortcomings in harmonised standards or common specifications conferring a presumption of conformity [Art. 79(6)(c)];
  * Non-compliance with Article 50 [Art. 79(6)(d)].
* **Failure to take corrective actions:** When an operator fails to take adequate corrective action within the prescribed period (within the shorter of 15 working days, or as provided for in relevant Union harmonisation legislation) after being evaluated and found non-compliant [Art. 79(2), Art. 79(5)].
* **Misclassification as non-high-risk:** When a provider has classified an AI system as non-high-risk pursuant to Article 6(3), but the authority has sufficient reason to consider that it is indeed high-risk, and confirms through evaluation that it is high-risk [Art. 80(1), Art. 80(2)].
* **Persistent formal non-compliance:** When formal non-compliance referred to in Article 83(1) persists [Art. 83(2)].

The provided provisions do not address mandatory warranties for AI-enabled smart fridges (or any consumer warranty periods). 

The provided excerpts instead cover:
* The subject matter and scope of the Regulation [Art. 1(2)];
* Obligations to keep documents (such as the EU declaration of conformity, certificates, and technical documentation) at the disposal of authorities for 10 years [Art. 47(1)], [Art. 22(3)(b)], [Art. 23(5)];
* The validity and extension periods for certificates of high-risk AI systems [Art. 44(2)];
* Timeframes for national market surveillance procedures and objections [Art. 79(8)]; and
* Transition periods, entry into force, and application dates of the Regulation [Art. 111(3)], [Art. 113].

The provided provisions do not address or set any royalty rates for artists whose works are used to train AI models. 

Instead, the provided excerpts cover:
* The general purpose of the Regulation [Art. 1(1)];
* The requirement for providers of general-purpose AI models to put in place a policy to comply with Union copyright law—including identifying and complying with reservations of rights expressed under Article 4(3) of Directive (EU) 2019/790—and to publish a sufficiently detailed summary of the training content [Art. 53(1)(c), (d)];
* Principles regarding copyright, text and data mining exceptions, and the need to obtain rightsholder authorization when an opt-out has been expressly reserved [Recital 105];
* The definition of "training data" [Art. 3(29)];
* Prohibitions on untargeted scraping of facial images [Art. 5(1)(e)];
* The definition of "AI literacy" [Art. 3(56)];
* Amendments to Directive 2014/90/EU [Art. 105]; and
* Criteria for designating general-purpose AI models with systemic risk [Annex XIII].

Yes. Providers of high-risk AI systems are required to put a quality management system in place that ensures compliance with the Regulation [Art. 17(1)(a)]. 

This system must be documented "in a systematic and orderly manner in the form of written policies, procedures and instructions" [Art. 17(1)(a)], and it must cover at least the following aspects:

* A strategy for regulatory compliance, including conformity assessment procedures and management of modifications [Art. 17(1)(a)];
* Techniques, procedures, and systematic actions for the design, design control, and design verification of the system [Art. 17(1)(b)];
* Techniques, procedures, and systematic actions for development, quality control, and quality assurance [Art. 17(1)(c)];
* Systems and procedures for data management (such as data collection, labelling, storage, retention, etc.) performed before and for placing on the market or putting into service [Art. 17(1)(f)];
* The risk management system referred to in Article 9 [Art. 17(1)(g)];
* Setting up, implementing, and maintaining a post-market monitoring system in accordance with Article 72 [Art. 17(1)(h)];
* Systems and procedures for record-keeping of relevant documentation and information [Art. 17(1)(k)];
* An accountability framework detailing the responsibilities of management and staff regarding all of these aspects [Art. 17(1)(m)].

Under the EU AI Act, high-risk AI systems must meet the following human oversight requirements:

* **Design and Purpose:** Systems must be designed and developed—including with appropriate human-machine interface tools—so that they can be effectively overseen by natural persons while in use [Art. 14(1)]. The oversight aims to prevent or minimise risks to health, safety, or fundamental rights emerging from intended use or reasonably foreseeable misuse, particularly where risks persist despite other requirements [Art. 14(2)].
* **Types of Oversight Measures:** Measures must be commensurate with the risks, level of autonomy, and context of use, and must be ensured through either or both [Art. 14(3)]:
  * Measures built into the system by the provider before placing it on the market or putting it into service (when technically feasible) [Art. 14(3)(a)];
  * Measures identified by the provider prior to market placement or putting into service that are appropriate for the deployer to implement [Art. 14(3)(b)].
* **Enabling the Overseer:** The system must be provided to deployers such that individuals assigned to oversight are enabled, as appropriate and proportionate, to [Art. 14(4)]:
  * Understand the system’s capacities and limitations, and duly monitor operation to detect and address anomalies, dysfunctions, and unexpected performance [Art. 14(4)(a)];
  * Remain aware of automation bias (the tendency to automatically rely or over-rely on system outputs), especially where systems provide information or recommendations [Art. 14(4)(b)];
  * Correctly interpret system outputs, taking into account available interpretation tools and methods [Art. 14(4)(c)];
  * Decide to not use the system, or to disregard, override, or reverse its output in any situation [Art. 14(4)(d)];
  * Intervene in operation or interrupt the system via a "stop" button or similar procedure to bring it to a safe halt [Art. 14(4)(e)].
* **Deployer Obligations:** Deployers must assign oversight to natural persons who possess the necessary competence, training, authority, and support [Art. 26(2)].
* **Instructions for Use:** Instructions for use provided to deployers must include the human oversight measures, including technical measures facilitating output interpretation [Art. 13(3)(d)].
* **Specific Rule for Point 1(a) of Annex III Systems:** For these systems, deployers must not take action or decisions based on the system's identification unless it has been separately verified and confirmed by at least two natural persons with the necessary competence, training, and authority [Art. 14(5)]. This two-person verification requirement does not apply to systems used for law enforcement, migration, border control, or asylum where Union or national law considers it disproportionate [Art. 14(5)].

Selon l'AI Act, un système d'IA est considéré comme à haut risque (« high-risk ») s'il répond aux critères définis à l'Article 6 :

1. **Systèmes d'IA liés à des produits réglementés de l'Union** : Un système d'IA est considéré comme à haut risque lorsque les deux conditions suivantes sont réunies [Art. 6(1)] :
   - Le système d'IA est destiné à être utilisé comme composant de sécurité d'un produit, ou est lui-même un produit, couvert par la législation d'harmonisation de l'Union figurant à l'annexe I [Art. 6(1)(a)] ;
   - Le produit dont le système d'IA est un composant de sécurité, ou le système d'IA lui-même en tant que produit, est soumis à une évaluation de la conformité par un tiers en vue de sa mise sur le marché ou de sa mise en service conformément à cette législation [Art. 6(1)(b)]. Les produits visés comprennent notamment les machines, jouets, ascenseurs, dispositifs médicaux ou l'automobile [Recital 50].

2. **Systèmes d'IA énumérés à l'Annexe III** : En plus de ceux mentionnés ci-dessus, les systèmes d'IA visés à l'Annexe III sont considérés comme à haut risque [Art. 6(2)], [Annex III]. 
   - Par exemple, dans le domaine de l'accès et du bénéfice des services privés essentiels et des services et prestations publics essentiels [Annex III, point 5] :
     - Les systèmes utilisés pour évaluer l'éligibilité des personnes physiques aux prestations et services d'aide publique essentiels (y compris la santé), ou pour les octroyer, réduire, révoquer ou récupérer [Annex III, point 5(a)] ;
     - Les systèmes servant à évaluer la solvabilité ou établir la notation de crédit de personnes physiques (sauf détection des fraudes financières) [Annex III, point 5(b)] ;
     - Les systèmes servant à l'évaluation des risques et à la tarification concernant les personnes physiques en assurance vie et santé [Annex III, point 5(c)] ;
     - Les systèmes servant à évaluer/classer les appels d'urgence ou à réguler/établir la priorité dans l'envoi des services d'intervention d'urgence (police, pompiers, secours médicaux, tri des patients) [Annex III, point 5(d)].

**Exception et documentation** :
Un fournisseur qui estime qu'un système d'IA visé à l'Annexe III n'est pas à haut risque doit documenter son évaluation avant la mise sur le marché ou la mise en service, et est soumis à l'obligation d'enregistrement prévue à l'article 49(2) [Art. 6(4)].

According to the EU AI Act, a ‘general-purpose AI model’ is defined as an AI model that:

* displays significant generality [Art. 3(63)];
* is capable of competently performing a wide range of distinct tasks regardless of the way the model is placed on the market [Art. 3(63)];
* can be integrated into a variety of downstream systems or applications [Art. 3(63)]; and
* includes models trained with a large amount of data using self-supervision at scale [Art. 3(63)].

**Exception:** It does not include AI models that are used for research, development, or prototyping activities before they are placed on the market [Art. 3(63)].

Under the EU Artificial Intelligence Act, an ‘AI regulatory sandbox’ is defined as a controlled framework set up by a competent authority which offers providers or prospective providers of AI systems the possibility to develop, train, validate and test—where appropriate in real-world conditions—an innovative AI system, pursuant to a sandbox plan for a limited time under regulatory supervision [Art. 3(55)].

Key characteristics and functions established by the Act include:

* **Environment and Operation:** It provides a controlled environment that fosters innovation and facilitates the development, training, testing, and validation of innovative AI systems for a limited time before they are placed on the market or put into service, pursuant to a specific sandbox plan agreed between the providers (or prospective providers) and the competent authority [Art. 57(5)]. It may include supervised testing in real-world conditions [Art. 57(5)], subject to specifically agreed terms, conditions, and safeguards designed to protect fundamental rights, health, and safety [Art. 58(4)].
* **Objectives:** Establishing these sandboxes aims to:
  * Improve legal certainty to achieve compliance with the Regulation or other relevant Union and national law [Art. 57(9)(a)];
  * Support the sharing of best practices through cooperation with involved authorities [Art. 57(9)(b)];
  * Foster innovation and competitiveness and facilitate the development of an AI ecosystem [Art. 57(9)(c)];
  * Contribute to evidence-based regulatory learning [Art. 57(9)(d)];
  * Facilitate and accelerate access to the Union market, particularly for systems provided by SMEs, including start-ups [Art. 57(9)(e)].
* **Establishment and Competence:** Member States must ensure their competent authorities establish at least one operational sandbox at national level by 2 August 2026 (either independently, jointly with other Member States, or by participating in an existing sandbox providing equivalent national coverage) [Art. 57(1)]. In addition, the European Data Protection Supervisor may establish an AI regulatory sandbox for Union institutions, bodies, offices, and agencies [Art. 57(3)]. 

Later amendments to the Act are not reflected in this text.

Under the EU AI Act, record-keeping requirements for high-risk AI systems include technical event-logging capabilities, documentation retention obligations, and quality management procedures:

**1. Automatic Event Logging**
* High-risk AI systems must technically enable the automatic recording of events (logs) over their lifetime [Art. 12(1)].
* To ensure traceability appropriate to the system's intended purpose, logging capabilities must record events relevant for:
  * identifying situations that may present a risk within the meaning of Article 79(1) or lead to a substantial modification [Art. 12(2)(a)];
  * facilitating post-market monitoring under Article 72 [Art. 12(2)(b)]; and
  * monitoring operations referred to in Article 26(5) [Art. 12(2)(c)].
* Providers must keep the automatically generated logs when these are under their control [Art. 16(e)].
* For high-risk AI systems referred to in point 1(a) of Annex III, logging capabilities must provide, at a minimum [Art. 12(3)]:
  * the period of each use (start and end date and time) [Art. 12(3)(a)];
  * the reference database checked against input data [Art. 12(3)(b)];
  * the input data that produced a match [Art. 12(3)(c)]; and
  * the identification of natural persons involved in verifying results under Article 14(5) [Art. 12(3)(d)].

**2. Documentation Keeping**
* Providers must keep the following documentation at the disposal of national competent authorities for a period ending 10 years after the system is placed on the market or put into service [Art. 18(1)]:
  * technical documentation under Article 11 [Art. 18(1)(a)];
  * quality management system documentation under Article 17 [Art. 18(1)(b)];
  * documentation regarding changes approved by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(c)];
  * decisions and other documents issued by notified bodies, where applicable [Art. 18(1)(d)];
  * the EU declaration of conformity under Article 47 [Art. 18(1)(e)].
* Providers that are financial institutions subject to internal governance requirements under Union financial services law must maintain the technical documentation as part of documentation kept under that law [Art. 18(3)].

**3. Quality Management System**
* Providers must include "systems and procedures for record-keeping of all relevant documentation and information" within their written quality management system [Art. 17(1)(k)].

Under the EU AI Act, the role of the European Artificial Intelligence Board (the ‘Board’) is to "advise and assist the Commission and the Member States in order to facilitate the consistent and effective application of this Regulation" [Art. 66(e)], [Art. 66(f)]. 

To achieve this, the Board's tasks and functions include:

* **Issuing recommendations and written opinions:** Either at the Commission's request or on its own initiative, the Board may issue recommendations and written opinions on relevant implementation and application matters [Art. 66(e)]. This includes topics such as:
  * The development and application of codes of conduct, codes of practice, and Commission guidelines [Art. 66(e)(i)];
  * The evaluation and review of the Regulation, serious incident reports, the functioning of the EU database, delegated or implementing acts, and alignment with Union harmonisation legislation listed in Annex I [Art. 66(e)(ii)];
  * Technical specifications, existing standards regarding Chapter III, Section 2 requirements, and the use of harmonised standards or common specifications [Art. 66(e)(iii)], [Art. 66(e)(iv)];
  * Trends in European global competitiveness in AI, AI uptake, digital skills development, and evolving AI value chain typologies regarding accountability implications [Art. 66(e)(v)], [Art. 66(e)(vi)];
  * Potential needs to amend Annex III or revise Article 5 based on available evidence and technology developments [Art. 66(e)(vii)].
* **Promoting awareness and literacy:** Supporting the Commission in promoting AI literacy, public awareness, and understanding of the benefits, risks, safeguards, rights, and obligations concerning AI systems [Art. 66(f)].
* **Facilitating cooperation through sub-groups:** Establishing two standing sub-groups to provide a platform for cooperation and exchange between market surveillance authorities (acting as the administrative cooperation group/ADCO) and notifying authorities regarding notified bodies, as well as creating other standing or temporary sub-groups to examine specific issues [Art. 65(6)].

When applying to a notified body for the assessment of technical documentation under the conformity assessment procedure in Annex VII, the application lodged by the provider must include the following [Annex VII, point 4.1], [Annex VII, point 4.2]:

* The name and address of the provider [Annex VII, point 4.2(a)];
* A written declaration that the same application has not been lodged with any other notified body [Annex VII, point 4.2(b)];
* The technical documentation referred to in Annex IV [Annex VII, point 4.2(c)].

Regarding the technical documentation itself, it must contain at least the elements specified in Annex IV [Art. 11(1)], [Annex IV], including:
* Validation and testing procedures, data characteristics, metrics, test logs, signed test reports, and cybersecurity measures put in place [Annex IV, point 2(g)–(h)];
* A copy of the EU declaration of conformity [Annex IV, points 8–9];
* A detailed description of the post-market performance evaluation system, including the post-market monitoring plan [Annex IV, points 8–9].

*(Note: SMEs, including start-ups, may provide the Annex IV technical documentation elements in a simplified manner using a form established by the Commission, which notified bodies must accept [Art. 11(1)].)*

High-risk AI systems must be accompanied by instructions for use provided "in an appropriate digital format or otherwise that include concise, complete, correct and clear information that is relevant, accessible and comprehensible to deployers" [Art. 13(2)]. 

The instructions for use must contain at least the following information:

* **Provider details:** The identity and contact details of the provider and, where applicable, its authorised representative [Art. 13(3)(a)].
* **System characteristics, capabilities, and performance limitations:**
  * Its intended purpose [Art. 13(3)(b)(i)];
  * The level of accuracy, including metrics, robustness, and cybersecurity against which it has been tested and validated and which can be expected, as well as any known and foreseeable circumstances that may impact those expected levels [Art. 13(3)(b)(ii)], [Art. 15(3)];
  * Any known or foreseeable circumstances—related to its intended purpose or reasonably foreseeable misuse—that may lead to risks to health and safety or fundamental rights [Art. 13(3)(b)(iii)];
  * Where applicable, technical capabilities and characteristics to provide information relevant to explaining its output [Art. 13(3)(b)(iv)];
  * When appropriate, performance regarding specific persons or groups of persons on which it is intended to be used [Art. 13(3)(b)(v)];
  * When appropriate, specifications for input data or relevant information on the training, validation, and testing data sets used [Art. 13(3)(b)(vi)];
  * Where applicable, information enabling deployers to interpret and appropriately use the output [Art. 13(3)(b)(vii)].
* **Pre-determined changes:** Any changes to the system and its performance pre-determined by the provider at the time of the initial conformity assessment [Art. 13(3)(c)].
* **Human oversight measures:** The human oversight measures referred to in Article 14, including technical measures implemented to help deployers interpret the system's outputs [Art. 13(3)(d)].
* **Resources and maintenance:** Computational and hardware resources needed, expected lifetime, and necessary maintenance and care measures (including frequency and software updates) to ensure proper functioning [Art. 13(3)(e)].
* **Logging mechanisms:** Where relevant, a description of mechanisms within the system allowing deployers to properly collect, store, and interpret logs in accordance with Article 12 [Art. 13(3)(f)].
